f german forces  which  took place on may 2. and dulles was  pretty  fired up about that  he had achieved quite a feat  fortunately a sort of a lot of the  accomplishment  diminished when the entire german army  in europe surrendered  five days later  but it still was meaningful  wolff  had no doubt treated american prisoners  of war  better than he would have and it refused  to destroy italian artworks or italian  factories because he was talking to  dulles and and it did end  fighting five days early and you know  probably saved hundreds if not thousands  of  american lives  dulles stayed on in germany  after the war he became the station  chief for the oss in wiesbaden which is  just up the rhine a little bit from  frankfort  um  but his heart really wasn't in it  he kind of disagreed with american  occupation policy the americans  after the war had laid down very strict  rules that basically barred anybody who  had been associated with the nazi party  from participating in occupation  government  and dulles really didn't agree with that  policy his prime concern as always was  the soviet union and he wanted to make  sure that germany really got back on its  feet and was stable politically and  economically and if you had to turn to  ex-nazis to achieve that that was  the price you had to pay i mean he once  said  that  you can't even make the trains run in  germany on time without the help of  ex-nazis  they'll spend a lot of time  sort of not really managing the staff  that was assigned to him  and most of his attention was given to  what he called the crown jewels  these were people who he had worked with  during the war he wanted to make sure  that they were well looked after  kolba who we saw a few minutes ago was  particularly um  well looked after he was given a car and  food rations and you know basically  nobody had a car in germany right after  the war so that was that was something  um  dulles eventually he left germany in the  fall of 1945 when the os